37. What is React Fibre ?
-> Before Fiber, React used the "Stack Reconciler".
- It processed updates synchronously and recursively down the component tree.
- Once a render started, it could not be stopped until it completed.
- If a component tree was deep, a complex render would block the browser's single main thread. This caused noticeable UI lag, dropped animation frames, and frozen input fields

Fiber changed the Virtual DOM structure from a basic tree into a singly linked list of "fiber nodes". Each fiber node represents a "unit of work" corresponding to a React component. Because it uses a linked list instead of a recursive call stack, React can pause, resume, or discard rendering work at any time.
a. The Two-Phase Architecture
- The Render Phase (Asynchronous & Interruptible): React traverses the fiber tree and computes what changes are needed. If a high-priority task (like a user typing or an animation) comes in mid-render, React will pause this phase, handle the user input, and then resume or restart the render phase.
- The Commit Phase (Synchronous & Uninterruptible): Once the entire render phase finishes, React enters the commit phase to apply those calculated changes directly to the real DOM in a single, fast pass. This ensures the UI never looks partially updated

38. What is batching ?
-> Batching is a performance optimization feature in React that groups multiple state updates together into a single re-render of your component.
Instead of triggering a brand-new render for every single state update, React waits until all state changes inside an event handler are processed before updating the UI.

// React 17 behavior:
setTimeout(() => {
  setCount(c => c + 1); // Causes Render #1
  setFlag(f => !f);    // Causes Render #2
}, 1000);

// React 18+ behavior (Automatic Batching):
setTimeout(() => {
  setCount(c => c + 1); 
  setFlag(f => !f);    
  // React batches these into exactly ONE re-render!
}, 1000);

How to Opt-Out of Batching
-> If your application absolutely requires a state update to immediately force a DOM render before the next line of code runs, you can opt-out of batching using flushSync from the react-dom package.

import { flushSync } from 'react-dom';

function handleClick() {
  flushSync(() => {
    setCount(c => c + 1); // Forces an immediate, separate re-render
  });
  setFlag(f => !f); // Flushes independently later
}

Note: Using flushSync is generally discouraged because it hurts performance. It should only be used when dealing with direct DOM measurements or third-party non-React libraries.